 {
  "id": "h03_hippodrome",
  "title": "At the horse races, in the rain",
  "source": "Homily 58 on John, 4 (NPNF 1/14)",
  "text": "If a harper, or dancer, or stage player call the city, they all run eagerly, and spend the half of an entire day attending to him alone. But when God speaks to us by Prophets and Apostles, we yawn, we scratch ourselves, we are drowsy. Yet at horse races, though there is no roof over them to keep off the wet, while heavy rains are falling, and the wind is dashing the water into their faces, they stand like madmen. But here, where there are roofs overhead, and where the warmth is admirable, they hold back instead of running together.",
